        {
          "kind": "ASSURANCE_DESIGN_REJECTED",
          "attempt": 2,
          "reason": "Independent probe critique: Plan is largely source-grounded (EXPORT_STATES, validate_rows, detached snapshots, row copying, lock-free exporter, shared store, cancel-wins all exercised against real public entrypoints), but it has concrete HIGH-risk coverage gaps and one vacuous check. (1) No obligation or probe covers the store's FAILED transition: process_pending(exporter=fn) where fn raises is never exercised, yet the task explicitly requires the failed transition and SPEC.md states 'Exporter exceptions become job failures'. (2) The positive retry path is untested: no job is ever put into FAILED and retried to PENDING. probe_2 only checks retry on a SUCCEEDED job returns 409, so behavior_6's 'retry changes only FAILED jobs to PENDING, retaining their ID and original input and resetting progress/error' is only half verified; the retention-of-original-input and progress/error reset are unprobed. Corrections: add a probe that submits rows, calls jobs.process_pending(exporter=<function that raises>), asserts state==FAILED and an error field/progress, then dispatch POST /exports/{id}/retry and assert state==PENDING with the same id and original input (progress reset), then jobs.process_pending() and assert state==SUCCEEDED progress==100 and result csv == jobs.export_csv(submitted rows). (3) probe_1's behavior_8 check is vacuous with respect to the obligation: sys.version_info >= (3,11) plus successful imports does not evidence 'standard library only'; replace it with an actual import-graph/AST check that atlas pulls in no non-stdlib top-level modules, or drop behavior_8 from probe_1 instead of claiming coverage. Minor: behavior_3's progress==100 on success and the optional error field are asserted nowhere. The three contrasts do reject their wrong implementations and no assertion falsely rejects a correct implementation, so the deficiencies are missing/weak coverage rather than unsupported expectations."
        }
